macOS: %gs:0x20, %gs:0x08 and %gs:0x10 are PE-owned while PE code runs (Tib.FiberData, StackBase,
StackLimit) and the host's while unix code runs (the pthread QoS slot, the errno pointer, the MIG
reply port); the PE ntdll reads the stack bounds from the slots.

Windows keeps NT_TIB.FiberData, StackBase and StackLimit at %gs:0x20, 0x08 and 0x10, and a program that
runs its own fiber system (Boost.Context style, Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024) writes all three on
every switch and reads them back. On this tree %gs is the host thread block for PE code too, where those
slots are the pthread QoS class, the errno pointer and the MIG reply port, and patch 0009 handed PE code
Wine's Tib.FiberData after every syscall (the sim died in its "FiberError" assertion at BOOT_INIT, M4,
macOS 27.0, engine r18, 2026-10-08), while the sim's stack base written into %gs:0x08 was read as the
errno pointer by the first unix routine that touched errno (ntdll.so, movl (%rax) after __error(),
rax = 0x15010000, 2026-10-09, gin/private/notes/msfs-2024-2026-10-08.md sections C, G and H).

Now %gs:0x20 takes turns like %gs:0x68 (0011): what PE code last left there goes into the TEB's own
Tib.FiberData on entry to unix code and the host's QoS value (host_qos at 0x340) comes back; the reverse on
every return to PE code. %gs:0x08 and %gs:0x10 keep the host's errno pointer and MIG reply port on both
sides (host_errno at 0x350, host_mig at 0x358) until PE code writes one of them: from then on that slot is
PE-owned the same way through Tib.StackBase and Tib.StackLimit (pe_slot_flags at 0x360 remembers which),
and when PE code writes the host's value back (its own context switch saved what it found in the slot at
its first switch, so this is its return to the thread's own stack) the TEB field returns to the thread's
own bounds, kept in ntdll_thread_data pe_main_base and pe_main_limit (0x3d8, 0x3e0; the unix guard-page
move keeps the limit in step while the thread runs its own stack). Without that the TEB kept the last
fiber's bounds and the sim's __chkstk probed a fiber's limit page from the thread stack, an access
violation on a frame "outside the stack", dead at 3 s (r18s4, 2026-10-09).
PE mode cannot simply hold the TEB fields: D3DMetal's PE DLLs call host code directly on the PE stack, and
the form of this patch that did so killed steamwebhelper.exe on the first failing syscall (libsystem_kernel
wrote errno through %gs:0x08 = 0x110000, the thread's stack base, r18s3, 2026-10-09). The TEB fields are
the natural PE-side store: Wine's unix side and kernelbase's fibers already read and write them, and
ntoskrnl keeps its KTHREAD in teb->Instrumentation[1], which an earlier form overwrote (winedevice.exe read
[StackBase+0x18] and died at every boot, r18ps and r18s2).

Two rules make the swaps hold at every instruction: the entry swap runs on the kernel stack and the return
swap on the user stack, so "%rsp inside the syscall region" and "the slots are the host's" always agree,
and every swap is compare-based, each slot moved only while it still holds the other side's value, so a
swap can run twice without stashing the wrong value. Signal handlers follow the same rules: init_handler
puts the host's values in (the handler is unix code: wait_suspend reads errno first thing), leave_handler
puts PE's back when the context it returns to is outside the signal stack and the syscall region, which
also covers a handler that redirected there (an exception to dispatch, a syscall fault returning to its
caller). user_mode_callback_return swaps nothing: NtCallbackReturn is a syscall and the dispatcher already
did it (the first trial saved the host's values as PE's there, and a second trial's signal handlers read
errno through PE's stack base: the 32-bit Steam died in wait_suspend, "nested exception on signal stack").

call_init_thunk keeps the host's values and clears the flags, the unix guard-page move writes
Tib.StackLimit as before, kernelbase's SwitchToFiber writes the slots as it writes the fields, and the PE
ntdll keeps reading the bounds from the TEB fields, which every crossing refreshes from the slots. A
program that writes its fiber's bounds into the slots makes D3DMetal's direct host calls on that thread
see them, as it did before this patch. Trial, branch msfs-gsbase. Fifth form, plus a trace channel hbstack (WINEDEBUG=+hbstack) on the
unix guard-page growth and on guard faults outside the thread stack, to see whether a fiber running off
its own stack is grown silently into its neighbour.
